p(7,2,"I was quite happy in my new place, and if there was one thing",
 "Black Beauty is happy in his new home, but he misses his freedom. A young horse full of life finds it hard to stand in a stable all day. John is patient with him.",
 "I was quite happy in my new place. If there was one thing I missed, you must not think that I was not happy. Everyone who had to do with me was good, I had a light stable with plenty of fresh air, and I had the best food. What more could I want? Freedom! For three and a half years of my life I had all the freedom I could wish for. But now, week after week and month after month, and surely year after year, I must stand in a stable night and day, except when I am wanted. And then I must be as steady and quiet as an old horse who has worked for twenty years. There are straps here and straps there, a bit in my mouth, and blinkers over my eyes. I am not complaining, because I know it must be so. I only want to say that it is hard for a young horse full of strength and spirit. He is used to a big field or open land, where he can throw up his head and his tail and gallop away at full speed, then turn and come back with a snort to his friends. I say it is hard never to have a little more freedom to do what you like. Sometimes, when I have had less exercise than usual, I feel so full of life that I cannot keep quiet when John takes me out. Whatever I do, it seems I must jump or dance or prance, and I know I gave him many a good shake, especially at the start. But he was always good and patient.",
 ["liberty","discontented","airy","steady","blinkers","fling","toss","gallop","snort","prance"])
s(7,"Spirited horses, when not enough exercised, are often called skittish",
 "Lively horses that do not get enough exercise are often called skittish, when really they are only playing.",
 "'when not enough exercised' is a short form of 'when they are not exercised enough'; the last 'when' means 'while in fact'.")
